#{[17592186115859 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is obtained by applying exactly one elementary row operation to the identity matrix $I_3$.\n\n- $A$ is obtained by $R_3:=R_3+5R_1$.\n- $C$ is obtained by $R_2:=R_2+5R_1$.\n- $B$ requires both adding a multiple of $R_1$ to $R_3$ and scaling the diagonal entry in $R_3$. One row swap, one row scaling, or one row addition cannot produce both changes from $I_3$.\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $C$ only are elementary matrices."] [17592186120312 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $q$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq a_2&=b-7a_1\\\\\nq\\begin{bmatrix}8\\\\6\\end{bmatrix}\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}32\\\\17\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}32\\\\24\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates give $q=4$."] [17592186120338 :question/worked-solution "We need a linearly independent subset that has the same span. Place the given vectors in the columns of a matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5&-1&2\\\\0&7&-14\\\\0&0&0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in row echelon form, with pivots in the first and second columns. The third column is $-2$ times the second, so $a_3=-2a_2$ can be removed without changing the span. Select the first and second columns from the original matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5\\\\0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\7\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186120351 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a-6x&=b\\\\\nx&=\\frac16(2a-b)\\\\\n&=\\frac16\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\\\10\\\\2\\\\6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8\\\\4\\\\-22\\\\-6\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac16\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\\\6\\\\24\\\\12\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\\\1\\\\4\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1+x_2=-2+1=-1$."] [17592186120364 :question/worked-solution "A matrix with rank $4$ must have at least four rows and four columns. This excludes $A$, $C$, and $E$, which have fewer than four rows or columns.\n\nThe remaining matrices are in row echelon form. Matrix $D$ has three nonzero rows, hence three pivot columns, so its rank is $3$. Matrix $B$ has four nonzero rows with pivots in columns $1$, $2$, $3$, and $4$, so its rank is $4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct matrix is $B$."] [17592186120366 :question/worked-solution "The rank of a matrix is the number of its pivot columns. Since $P$ has $5$ pivot columns,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{rank}(P)=5.\n$$"] [17592186120384 :question/worked-solution "The rank is the number of pivot columns in row echelon form.\n\nMatrix $A$ has only two rows, so its rank is at most $2$.\n\nFor $B$, apply $R_2:=R_2-3R_3$ to obtain a row with entries $0,0,0,-5,-1$. This row, together with the rows whose last two entries are $2,3$ and $0,3$, spans a two-dimensional space in columns $4$ and $5$. Along with the pivots in columns $1$ and $3$, this gives four pivot columns. Thus $\\operatorname{rank}(B)=4$.\n\nFor $C$, the second column is zero. The first, third, and fourth columns are independent: a $3\\times3$ minor using rows $1$, $2$, and $4$ has determinant $-2$. Hence $\\operatorname{rank}(C)=3$.\n\nTherefore, $B$ only has rank $4$."] [17592186120512 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying an $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ multiplies each of its $n$ rows by $k$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\det(kA)=k^n\\det(A).\n$$\n\nHere $k=5$, $n=3$, and $\\det(A)=4$, so\n\n$$\n\\det(5A)=5^3\\cdot4=125\\cdot4=500.\n$$"] [17592186120517 :question/worked-solution "A basis is a linearly independent set that spans the given space. By inspection,\n\n$$\na_1=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}\n=a_2+a_3.\n$$\n\nThus $a_1$ can be removed without changing the span. The vectors $a_2$ and $a_3$ are not parallel, so they are linearly independent. Therefore, a basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186120518 :question/worked-solution "A basis is a linearly independent set that spans the given space. By inspection,\n\n$$\na_3=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}\n=a_1+a_2.\n$$\n\nThus $a_3$ can be removed without changing the span. The vectors $a_1$ and $a_2$ are not parallel, so they are linearly independent. Therefore, a basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186120523 :question/worked-solution "We want to find coefficients $x_1,x_2$ such that $a_3=x_1a_1+x_2a_2$. Equating coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x_1-x_2&=2\\\\-2x_1+4x_2&=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdd twice the first equation to the second to obtain $2x_2=2$, so $x_2=1$. The first equation then gives $x_1=3$.\n\nThe coefficient matrix has a pivot in both columns, so $a_1$ and $a_2$ are linearly independent. Also $a_3=3a_1+a_2$, so they span the same space as the three given vectors. Thus $B$ is a basis, and\n\n$$\nx_1x_2=3\\cdot1=3.\n$$"] [17592186124900 :question/worked-solution "To test linear dependence, solve\n\n$$\nx_1p_1(t)+x_2p_2(t)=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given polynomials and collecting coefficients gives\n\n$$\n( x_1+2x_2 )+(-x_1-3x_2)t+x_1t^2=0.\n$$\n\nThis identity holds for all $t$ only if every coefficient is zero. The coefficient of $t^2$ gives $x_1=0$, and the constant coefficient then gives $2x_2=0$, so $x_2=0$.\n\nThe only solution is the zero solution. Therefore, the set is linearly independent; there is no nonzero solution from which to form the requested ratio."] [17592186125244 :question/worked-solution "Examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $c_1+c_2t+c_3t^3=0$ for every $t$, comparison of coefficients gives $c_1=c_2=c_3=0$. Thus $\\{1,t,t^3\\}$ is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is false. Every linear combination of $1,t,t^3$ has coefficient $0$ on $t^2$, whereas $-5t^3+t^2+t$ has coefficient $1$ on $t^2$.\n- Statement III is false. The set does not span $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ because, for example, it cannot represent $t^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only."] [17592186393605 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186393605 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{x}_{1}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{2}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}+{\\color{rblack}{x}_{3}}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3},{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}+2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{a}}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-5)+0+2=-3.$"] [17592186396585 :question/problem "The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ if\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186396585 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [17592186396694 :question/problem "The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ if\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186396694 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=4\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+(-1){\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [17592186396701 :question/problem "The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ if\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186396701 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 2{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [17592186399713 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection."] [17592186399713 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186399720 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection."] [17592186399720 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186400867 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain\n\n$$\n|A|=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has identical columns:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}-2} & 0 & {\\color{blue}-2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & 1 & {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & 0 & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, its determinant is:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=5\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot (-10)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [17592186402861 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|A|=3\\cdot (-6)\\cdot 2=-36\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}} & {\\color{blue}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{2}} & {\\color{blue}8} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|C|=(-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=-12\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ is equal to $0.$"] [17592186402868 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|B|=2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4=-40\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}-2} & 4 & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}6} & \\sqrt{3} & {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-4} & \\sqrt{7} & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [17592186404726 :question/problem "Consider the set $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+t+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)=0.$"] [17592186404726 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1+t+{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({x}_{2})t+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the third equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is linearly independent."]}
